.TH JN 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
jn \- create a directory junction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jn
.I dir junc
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jn
creates
.IR junc ,
a directory junction to the existing directory
.IR dir .
A junction is a reparse point that redirects a directory to another
directory on the same computer, so a path under
.I junc
names the same file as the path at the same place under
.IR dir .
Unlike a symbolic link, a junction needs no special privilege or system
setting to create.
.PP
.I dir
must exist and be a directory.
The junction stores the name that
.BR realpath (1)
prints for it.
A relative
.I dir
is taken from the current directory, not from the directory of
.IR junc .
The
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
components are removed by name before links are followed, so
.I link/..\&
is the directory that contains
.IR link ,
not the parent of its target.
Every symbolic link and junction is then replaced with its target, so
the junction never refers to another link, and each name gets the case
and the long form that it has on disk.
The resolved name must begin with a drive letter.
.PP
.I junc
must not exist, but its parent directory must.
.B jn
creates
.I junc
as an empty directory and then turns it into a junction.
If that fails, it removes
.I junc
again.
.PP
On success
.B jn
writes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.B jn
has no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
Any other argument that begins with
.BR \- ,
other than
.B \-
itself, is an unknown option and an error, even after the operands
unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a
.I junc
named
.B \-x
is created with
.BI "jn \-\- " dir " \-x\fR."
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and the arguments after it
are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows may show a critical-error dialog for a path on a drive with no
disk in it.
Otherwise busybox suppresses the dialog, and the operation fails.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options, so that a later argument
such as
.B \-x
is a name.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The junction was created, or
.B \-\-help
printed the usage message.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, or a
junction that could not be created.
.PP
A failure is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
jn: can\(aqt create junction \(aq\fIjunc\fR\(aq to \(aq\fIdir\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
with the operands as typed.
Common reasons are
.B No such file or directory
when
.I dir
or the parent of
.I junc
is missing,
.B Not a directory
when
.I dir
is a file,
.B File exists
when
.I junc
exists, and
.B Invalid argument
when
.I dir
resolves to a name without a drive letter, as a UNC path or a path on
a mapped network drive does.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a library directory available under a second name, then show where
the junction points:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ jn C:/src/zlib\-1.3.1 vendor/zlib
$ readlink vendor/zlib
C:/src/zlib\-1.3.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a directory whose name contains a space a path without one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ jn "C:/Program Files/Some Tool" C:/tools/some
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a junction, leaving its target directory alone:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm vendor/zlib
.EE
.RE
.PP
A junction cannot replace an existing file or directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ jn src build; echo $?
jn: can\(aqt create junction \(aqbuild\(aq to \(aqsrc\(aq: File exists
1
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from a symbolic link made by
.BR "ln \-s" :
.IP \(bu 2
The target must be a directory.
.IP \(bu 2
A relative target is taken from the current directory, not from the
directory of the junction.
.IP \(bu 2
The target is stored as an absolute path, so moving the junction does
not change where it points.
.IP \(bu 2
A UNC path such as
.I //server/share/dir
is rejected as a target, and so is a directory on a mapped network
drive, which resolves to a UNC path.
.PP
Other busybox programs treat a junction as a symbolic link.
.B ls \-l
shows it as
.IB junc " \-> " path
and
.B readlink
prints the stored path, with forward slashes.
.B rm
removes only the junction, with or without
.BR \-r ,
and leaves the directory it points to alone.
.B rmdir
refuses, with
.BR "Not a directory" .
In
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B dir
lists a junction as
.BR <JUNCTION> ,
and
.B rmdir
removes it.
.PP
.B jn
.I dir junc
is like the
.B cmd.exe
command
.B mklink /J
.IR "junc dir" ,
with the operands in the other order, except that
.B mklink
only makes
.I dir
absolute: it does not check that
.I dir
is an existing directory, and does not resolve links in it.
.PP
The volume that holds
.I junc
must support reparse points, as NTFS does.
.PP
Operands may use drive letters and either kind of slash.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The absolute name of
.I dir
is limited to 259 characters, and a longer one fails with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
When the resolved name would be longer than 255 characters, links are
not followed, and the junction stores the absolute name as typed.
.PP
On Windows XP, which lacks the API for finding a final name, a junction
in
.I dir
is followed only when it is the last component, and names keep the
case and form in which they were typed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR readlink (1),
.BR realpath (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR rmdir (1)
